# Getting started

The bootstrap created the repository, the pipelines and the Azure side. Nothing has run yet: the
starter files were committed with `[skip ci]`.

## 1. Check the pipelines

In Azure DevOps, project **${project_reference}**, Pipelines:

- `01 ${pipeline_display_name} Continuous Integration` - run it once by hand on `main`. It should pass, and its
  **Extensions** tab should show "No changes. Infrastructure matches the configuration."
- `02 ${pipeline_display_name} Continuous Delivery` - run it once. The plan is empty, so the **Apply** stage is skipped.

If a run stops with "needs permission to use ...", the pipeline was not yet authorised for that
resource: that is a bootstrap fault, not something to approve by hand - tell whoever ran the
bootstrap.

## 2. Make a first change

```pwsh
git clone ${organization_url}/${replace(project_reference, " ", "%20")}/_git/${repository_name}
cd ${repository_name}
git switch -c first-resource
```

Uncomment the example resource group in `main.tf`, commit, push, and open a pull request into
`main`. The CI run's **Extensions** tab shows `1 to add`. Complete the pull request (squash). The CD
run plans, asks the approvers, and applies.

## 3. Lock the provider versions

After your first local `terraform init`, commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`.

## Who approves

Approvers are the members of the Azure DevOps group `${approvers_group}`: %{ if length(approvers) + length(approver_groups) == 0 }**none - the bootstrap was run with no approvers, so an apply is not held for approval.**%{ else }%{ if length(approvers) > 0 }${join(", ", approvers)}%{ endif }%{ if length(approvers) > 0 && length(approver_groups) > 0 }, and %{ endif }%{ if length(approver_groups) > 0 }the members of the Entra group(s) ${join(", ", approver_groups)}%{ endif }.%{ endif }
